{
  "id": "COMPOUND:14268 - PROTEIN:1285",
  "verbalization": "Benzocaine is implicated in the regulation of RET proto-oncogene through a multi-step pathway involving K-252a, which acts as a target of multiple benzocaine-related entities. The evidence suggests that benzocaine interacts with a transporter protein, Solute carrier organic anion transporter family member 2A1, which in turn is targeted by K-252a. This chain of interactions ultimately leads to the activation of RET proto-oncogene. The shared involvement of K-252a in targeting RET across different targets (Glutamine--fructose-6-phosphate aminotransferase and HSVEC) indicates a potential mechanism by which benzocaine influences RET through a common intermediary. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of K-252a as a critical mediator in the benzocaine-RET relationship, suggesting that benzocaine may modulate RET activity via a transporter-dependent mechanism. The consistent presence of K-252a in the target list across different benzocaine-related entities supports the hypothesis that benzocaine exerts its effects on RET through a pathway involving K-252a, which may be a key player in the regulation of RET proto-oncogene.",
